,"lemma":"நெறி","gloss":"curled"}],"gloss":"curled"},{"surface":"தழை","parts":[{"text":"தழை","lemma":"தழை","gloss":"leaf-garment"}],"gloss":"leaf-garment"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"தித்தி","parts":[{"text":"தித்தி","lemma":"தித்தி","gloss":"beauty spots"}],"gloss":"beauty spots"},{"surface":"குறங்கின்","parts":[{"text":"குறங்கு","lemma":"குறங்கு","gloss":"thigh"},{"text":"இன்","lemma":"இன்","gloss":"on"}],"gloss":"on the thighs"},{"surface":"ஊழ்","parts":[{"text":"ஊழ்","lemma":"ஊழ்","gloss":"turn"}],"gloss":"in turn"},{"surface":"மாறு","parts":[{"text":"மாறு","lemma":"மாறு","gloss":"alternately"}],"gloss":"alternately"},{"surface":"அலைப்ப","parts":[{"text":"அலைப்ப","lemma":"அலைப்ப","gloss":"to brush against"}],"gloss":"brushing against"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"வருமே","parts":[{"text":"வரும்","lemma":"வருதல்","gloss":"comes"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"indeed"}],"gloss":"comes indeed"},{"surface":"சே","parts":[{"text":"சே","lemma":"சே","gloss":"red/gold"}],"gloss":"red-gold"},{"surface":"இழை","parts":[{"text":"இழை","lemma":"இழை","gloss":"jewels"}],"gloss":"jewels"},{"surface":"அந்தில்","parts":[{"text":"அந்தில்","lemma":"அந்தில்","gloss":"look there!"}],"gloss":"look there!"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கொழுநன்","parts":[{"text":"கொழுநன்","lemma":"கொழுநன்","gloss":"husband"}],"gloss":"husband"},{"surface":"காணிய","parts":[{"text":"காணிய","lemma":"காணல்","gloss":"to see"}],"gloss":"to see"},{"surface":"அளியேன்","parts":[{"text":"அளியேன்","lemma":"அளியேன்","gloss":"pitiable me"}],"gloss":"pitiable me"},{"surface":"மன்னே","parts":[{"text":"மன்","lemma":"மன்","gloss":"indeed"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"alas"}],"gloss":"alas!"}]}],"literal":"In the inner town of Āttirai, kinsman of Kaḷḷil, / the cotton-like beautiful small unripe fruit yielded by the spathe / of the tall dark palmyra palm, along with the tender fruit, returning / like the ancient town of Āti Arumaṉ, / the beautiful, contrasting, curled leaf-garment of white water-lilies from the pond, / brushing alternately in turn against her thighs with beauty spots, / she comes indeed, she of the red-gold jewels, look there! / To see my husband, pitiable am I, alas!","translation":"Look there! She of the red-gold jewels comes to see my husband. Her beautiful, contrasting, curled leaf-garment made of white water-lilies from the pond brushes alternately against her thighs adorned with beauty spots. She is as beautiful as the ancient town of Āti Arumaṉ, where people return carrying tender palmyra fruits along with the cotton-like small unripe fruits yielded by the spathes of the tall, dark palmyra trees that grow in the inner town of Āttirai, the kinsman of Kaḷḷil. Alas, how pitiable am I!","modern":"கள்ளில் ஊராரின் உறவினரான ஆத்திரையினது உள்ளூரில் உள்ள, உயர்ந்த கரிய பனைமரத்தின் பாளை தந்த பஞ்சு போன்ற சிறிய காய்களையும் நுங்கினையும் கொண்டு மக்கள் மீளும் ஆதி அருமனின் பழமையான ஊரைப் போன்றவள் அவள். குளத்து வெண் ஆம்பல் மலரின் அழகிய, நெருக்கமான, சுருண்ட தழையாடை, தேமல் படர்ந்த தன் தொடைகளில் மாறி மாறி மோதி அசைய, சிவந்த அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த பரத்தை அங்கே வருகிறாள்; என் கணவனைக் காண்பதற்காக! அந்தோ, நான் இரங்கத்தக்கவள் ஆயினேன்!","explanation":"This poem is spoken by the heroine (Talaivi) to her companion (Thozhi) upon seeing the courtesan (parattai) boldly walking down her street. The heroine describes the courtesan's striking appearance: she wears a beautiful, curled leaf-garment made of white water-lilies that brushes against her thighs as she walks. The courtesan is compared to the ancient, prosperous town of Āti Arumaṉ, which is rich with palmyra trees. The heroine laments her own helpless and pitiable state, as she must endure the humiliation of the courtesan coming openly to see her husband. This poem belongs to the Marutam tiṇai (agricultural landscape), which traditionally depicts the husband's infidelity and the wife's resulting grief and sulking.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}